obviously those of AS. ''biddan'', and others obviously those of AS. ''beódan'', no formal separation can conveniently be made. The mod. forms correspond to those of AS. ''biddan'', the senses chiefly to those of AS. ''beódan''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To ask; request; invite.
{{smaller block|Go ye therefore into the highways, and as many as ye shall find ''bid'' to the marriage. {{right|Mat. xxii. 9.}}}}
{{smaller block|Provide the feast, father, and ''bid'' the guests.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., ii. 1.}}}}
'''2.''' To pray; wish earnestly or devoutly; hence, to say by way of greeting or benediction: as, to ''bid'' good-day, farewell, etc.
{{smaller block|Neither ''bid'' him God speed. {{right|2 John 10.}}}}
'''3.''' To command; order or direct; enjoin.
{{smaller block|And Peter answered him and said, Lord, if it be thou, ''bid'' me come unto thee on the water. {{right|Mat. xiv. 28.}}}}
{{smaller block|I was ''bid'' to come for you. {{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|Because God his Father had not ''bidden'' him to do it, and therefore He would not tempt the Lord his God.
{{right|''Kingsley.''}}}}
{{smaller block|[Occasionally a simple infinitive follows: as, “the lady ''bade'' take away the fool,” ''Shak.'', T N., i. 5.]}}
'''4.''' To offer; propose: as, to ''bid'' a price at an auction.
{{smaller block|The king will ''bid'' you battle presently.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., v. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|Four guineas! Gad’s life, you don’t ''bid'' me the price of his wig. {{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iv. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|In buying Books or other Commodities, ’tis not always the best way to ''bid'' half so much as the seller asks.
{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 30.}}}}

